---
// The licence, where the code is, how to contribute, and the one part that is not Apache-2.0.
import { REPO_URL, repoFile } from "../../site";
---

<section id="open-source" class="split-text" aria-labelledby="open-source-title">
  <h2 id="open-source-title">Free and open source</h2>
  <div>
    <p class="note">
      Where it stands today: Apache-2.0, the command line out on npm and Homebrew, the apps building from the source
      with no release yet, and every version of the command line in <a href={repoFile("cli/CHANGELOG.md")}>the
      changelog</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Dayhand is licensed under the <a href={repoFile("LICENSE")}>Apache License 2.0</a>. The apps, the server, the
      command line, the documentation and this site live in one public repository,
      <a href={REPO_URL}>github.com/maiarowsky/dayhand</a>, and you may use them for your business, change them and share
      them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today there is no paid plan and no account to create. A hosted server, <a href="#dayhand-cloud">Dayhand Cloud</a>,
      is planned for $5 a month for people who would rather not run their own; running Dayhand yourself stays free.
    </p>
    <p>
      Contributions are accepted under the Developer Certificate of Origin
      (<a href={repoFile("CONTRIBUTING.md")}>how to contribute</a>). Security problems go privately, never in an issue
      (<a href={repoFile("SECURITY.md")}>security policy</a>).
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The Convex local backend that <code>dayhand server start</code> downloads on macOS and Linux is not part of
      Dayhand: it is source-available under FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0, fetched at a pinned version with a verified checksum.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
